Java's classic `switch` statement has been a source of bugs for 20+ years:

```java
// Classic switch — verbose, fall-through risk
String result;
switch (day) {
    case MONDAY:
    case TUESDAY:
    case WEDNESDAY:
    case THURSDAY:
    case FRIDAY:
        result = "Weekday";
        break;
    case SATURDAY:
    case SUNDAY:
        result = "Weekend";
        break;
    default:
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown: " + day);
}
```

**Problems**:
1. **Fall-through by default** — forgetting `break` causes silent bugs.
2. **Verbose** — each case label needs its own line.
3. **Statement, not expression** — must declare a variable outside, mutate inside.
4. **No exhaustiveness check** — missed cases compile fine.
5. **`default` always required** when used as expression-like assignment.

Java 14 introduced **switch expressions** (JEP 361) to fix all of these:

```java
String result = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, TUESDAY, WEDNESDAY, THURSDAY, FRIDAY -> "Weekday";
    case SATURDAY, SUNDAY                            -> "Weekend";
};
// No `break`. No fall-through. Exhaustive (all enum constants covered).
// `result` declared and assigned in one line.
```

**Single-value arrow case**:
```java
int days = switch (month) {
    case JAN, MAR, MAY, JUL, AUG, OCT, DEC -> 31;
    case APR, JUN, SEP, NOV                -> 30;
    case FEB                               -> isLeap ? 29 : 28;
};
```

**Each case is independent** — no fall-through, no `break` needed. Multiple labels separated by commas group the same body.

**Switch expression in `return`**:
```java
public String dayCategory(DayOfWeek day) {
    return switch (day) {
        case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> "Weekend";
        default              -> "Weekday";
    };
}
```

**Switch expression in field initializer**:
```java
private final String mode = switch (System.getenv("ENV")) {
    case "prod" -> "PRODUCTION";
    case "stg"  -> "STAGING";
    case null   -> "DEV";  // Java 21 — case null
    default     -> "DEV";
};
```

**Switch expression as method argument**:
```java
logger.info("Status: {}", switch (status) {
    case OK    -> "all good";
    case ERROR -> "problem";
    case BUSY  -> "throttled";
});
```

When a case needs multiple statements (logging, validation, computation), use a **block** with **`yield`** to return the value:

```java
int numLetters = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY, FRIDAY, SUNDAY -> 6;
    case TUESDAY                -> 7;
    case THURSDAY, SATURDAY     -> 8;
    case WEDNESDAY              -> {
        log.info("Wednesday has the most letters");
        int count = day.name().length();
        yield count;  // returns from this case block
    }
};
```

**`yield` vs `return`**:
- **`yield value;`** — returns a value from the switch case block to the surrounding switch expression.
- **`return value;`** — returns a value from the entire enclosing method.
- They're not interchangeable.

**`yield` is also valid in colon-style switch expressions** (introduced for backward-compatible authors):
```java
// Colon-style switch expression with yield (less common; arrow is preferred)
int x = switch (day) {
    case MONDAY:
        yield 1;
    case TUESDAY:
        yield 2;
    default:
        yield 0;
};
```
Most modern code uses arrow syntax — colon-with-yield is mainly for migration of legacy patterns.

**Mixing arrow and colon cases in one switch is a compile error** — pick one style per switch.

Switch expressions **must be exhaustive** — the compiler verifies all possible inputs are covered. If not, you get a compile error.

**For enums** — listing every constant satisfies exhaustiveness; no `default` needed:
```java
enum Status { OK, ERROR, BUSY }

String msg = switch (status) {
    case OK    -> "all good";
    case ERROR -> "problem";
    case BUSY  -> "throttled";
    // No default needed — all enum constants covered
};
```

**Add a new enum constant later → compile error in every switch**:
```java
enum Status { OK, ERROR, BUSY, MAINTENANCE }  // added MAINTENANCE

String msg = switch (status) {
    case OK    -> "all good";
    case ERROR -> "problem";
    case BUSY  -> "throttled";
    // COMPILE ERROR: MAINTENANCE not handled
};
```
This is gold for evolving codebases — the compiler tells you every place that needs updating.

**For sealed types (Java 17+)** — listing every permitted subtype is exhaustive:
```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}

double area = switch (shape) {
    case Circle c   -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
    case Square s   -> s.side() * s.side();
    case Triangle t -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
    // No default needed — sealed hierarchy is closed
};
```

**For non-enum/non-sealed types** — you must include `default`:
```java
int result = switch (someInt) {
    case 0 -> 0;
    case 1 -> 10;
    case 2 -> 20;
    default -> -1;  // required — the compiler can't enumerate all int values
};
```

**Implicit `default` in old-style statement** is also gone — no warning if you forget. Switch **expression** is strict; switch **statement** is still lenient (for backward compat).

You can use the arrow syntax in **switch statements** too — not just expressions:
```java
switch (event.type()) {
    case CLICK    -> handleClick(event);
    case HOVER    -> handleHover(event);
    case SUBMIT   -> handleSubmit(event);
    default       -> log.warn("Unknown event: {}", event);
}
// No assignment, no return value; just side effects.
// Still no fall-through; still no `break` needed.
```

**Why use arrow-style statement?**
- Same fall-through-free safety as expression.
- No need to add `break` to every case.
- Cleaner than the colon style for side-effect-only code.

**When to use which**:
- **Switch expression with arrow** — when you want a value (most cases).
- **Switch statement with arrow** — when you only need side effects (event dispatch).
- **Switch statement with colon + break** — only if you specifically need fall-through (rare and usually a smell).

**Forbidden mix**:
```java
// COMPILE ERROR: cannot mix arrow and colon
switch (x) {
    case 1 -> doOne();
    case 2: doTwo(); break;  // ERROR
}
```

Switch expressions set the stage for **Java 21 pattern matching for switch (JEP 441)** — the major payoff.

**Type patterns**:
```java
String describe(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Integer i -> "int: " + i;
        case Long l    -> "long: " + l;
        case String s  -> "string of length " + s.length();
        case null      -> "null";
        default        -> "other: " + obj;
    };
}
```

**Record deconstruction patterns (JEP 440, Java 21)**:
```java
record Point(int x, int y) {}
record Line(Point start, Point end) {}

double length = switch (shape) {
    case Line(Point(int x1, int y1), Point(int x2, int y2)) ->
        Math.hypot(x2 - x1, y2 - y1);
    case Point p -> 0.0;
    case null    -> Double.NaN;
    default      -> -1.0;
};
```

**`when` guards**:
```java
String classify = switch (number) {
    case Integer i when i < 0  -> "negative";
    case Integer i when i == 0 -> "zero";
    case Integer i when i < 10 -> "small positive";
    case Integer i             -> "large positive";
    case null                  -> "null";
    default                    -> "not an integer";
};
```

**Sealed types + pattern switch = exhaustive ADT-style matching**:
```java
sealed interface Result<T> permits Result.Success, Result.Failure {
    record Success<T>(T value) implements Result<T> {}
    record Failure<T>(String error) implements Result<T> {}
}

String render = switch (result) {
    case Result.Success<?>(var v) -> "ok: " + v;
    case Result.Failure<?>(var e) -> "error: " + e;
    // No default — sealed hierarchy is exhaustive
};
```

This is the deeper reason switch expressions matter: they're **the path to functional-style pattern matching** in Java.

Before Java 16, type-checking and downcasting looked like this:

```java
Object obj = "hello";
if (obj instanceof String) {
    String s = (String) obj;  // redundant cast!
    System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
}
```

The cast is redundant because the compiler just proved `obj` is a `String`. But Java's type system didn't carry that information into the body. You had to:
1. Type the check.
2. Duplicate the type name in the cast.
3. Declare a new variable.
4. Risk typos and inconsistencies.

Worse, for non-trivial names it got verbose:
```java
if (event instanceof OrderConfirmedEvent) {
    OrderConfirmedEvent confirmed = (OrderConfirmedEvent) event;
    processConfirmation(confirmed);
}
```

Java 16's pattern matching for `instanceof` fixes this.

```java
Object obj = "hello";
if (obj instanceof String s) {
    System.out.println(s.toUpperCase());
}
```

`s` is the **pattern variable** — declared and bound in the same expression.

**Works with any reference type**:
```java
if (event instanceof OrderConfirmedEvent confirmed) {
    processConfirmation(confirmed);
}

if (payload instanceof Map<?, ?> map) {
    map.forEach((k, v) -> ...);
}
```

**Negated form — guards on the false branch**:
```java
if (!(obj instanceof String s)) {
    return 0;
}
System.out.println(s.length());
```
This is **flow scoping** — the compiler tracks where the pattern variable is definitely bound based on control flow.

The pattern variable is **in scope wherever the compiler can prove the `instanceof` check succeeded**. This is a flow-sensitive analysis, more powerful than simple block scoping.

**Inside the true branch**:
```java
if (obj instanceof String s) {
    System.out.println(s.length());
}
```

**Short-circuited AND — `&&` extends scope**:
```java
if (obj instanceof String s && s.length() > 5) {
    System.out.println(s);
}
```
The RHS of `&&` can use `s` because the LHS was true.

**Short-circuited OR — `||` does NOT extend scope**:
```java
if (obj instanceof String s || s.length() > 5) {
    // ERROR: `s` not in scope — if LHS was false, `s` was never bound
}
```

**Negation + early return**:
```java
public int lengthOrZero(Object obj) {
    if (!(obj instanceof String s)) return 0;
    return s.length();
}
```

**Negation + throw**:
```java
if (!(obj instanceof User u)) {
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("not a user");
}
process(u);
```

**Else branch after positive match** — NOT in scope:
```java
if (obj instanceof String s) {
    System.out.println(s);
} else {
    // `s` NOT in scope here
}
```

**Cleaner `equals` implementations**:
```java
@Override
public boolean equals(Object other) {
    return other instanceof User u
        && Objects.equals(id, u.id)
        && Objects.equals(email, u.email);
}
```

**Type dispatch in event handlers**:
```java
void handle(Event event) {
    if (event instanceof OrderPlacedEvent placed) {
        onOrderPlaced(placed);
    } else if (event instanceof OrderShippedEvent shipped) {
        onOrderShipped(shipped);
    } else if (event instanceof OrderDeliveredEvent delivered) {
        onOrderDelivered(delivered);
    }
}
```

**Combined with negation for early return**:
```java
public BigDecimal totalOrZero(Object entity) {
    if (!(entity instanceof Order order)) return BigDecimal.ZERO;
    return order.getTotal();
}
```

**Nested checks with `&&`**:
```java
if (obj instanceof List<?> list && !list.isEmpty() && list.get(0) instanceof String first) {
    System.out.println("first item: " + first);
}
```

**Generic types**:
```java
if (obj instanceof Map<?, ?> map) {
    map.forEach((k, v) -> System.out.println(k + "=" + v));
}
```
Pattern matching can't introspect generic type parameters (type erasure), so you must use `<?>`.

The compiler rejects patterns that can never bind:

**Redundant same-type check**:
```java
if (x instanceof String s) {
    // ...
} else if (x instanceof String t) {
    // COMPILE ERROR: `t` can never bind (x is not a String here)
}
```

**Unreachable pattern after more specific**:
```java
if (x instanceof CharSequence cs) {
    // ...
} else if (x instanceof String s) {
    // COMPILE ERROR: String extends CharSequence; unreachable
}
```

**Already-bound variable clash**:
```java
String s = "foo";
if (obj instanceof String s) {  // ERROR: `s` already in scope
    // ...
}
```

`instanceof` pattern matching is just the first step. Later JEPs built on it:

**Switch pattern matching (Java 21, JEP 441)**:
```java
String describe(Object obj) {
    return switch (obj) {
        case Integer i -> "int: " + i;
        case String s  -> "string of length " + s.length();
        case int[] a   -> "array of length " + a.length;
        case null      -> "null";
        default        -> "other: " + obj;
    };
}
```

**Record deconstruction patterns (Java 21, JEP 440)**:
```java
record Point(int x, int y) {}
record Line(Point start, Point end) {}

if (obj instanceof Line(Point(int x1, int y1), Point(int x2, int y2))) {
    double length = Math.sqrt(Math.pow(x2 - x1, 2) + Math.pow(y2 - y1, 2));
}
```

**Guards with `when` (Java 21)**:
```java
return switch (shape) {
    case Circle c when c.radius() > 100 -> "large circle";
    case Circle c                       -> "small circle";
    case Square s when s.side() > 100   -> "large square";
    case Square s                       -> "small square";
};
```

**Sealed types + switch patterns = exhaustive matching with compile-time checks**:
```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}

String classify(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c   -> "circle";
        case Square sq  -> "square";
        case Triangle t -> "triangle";
    };
}
```

Java does **not** have string interpolation in text blocks (the **string templates** JEP was withdrawn in Java 23 due to design concerns). Instead, use **`String.formatted(args...)`** — added in Java 15 as a fluent alternative to `String.format`:

```java
String name = "Alice";
int count = 5;

String json = """
        {
          "name": "%s",
          "messageCount": %d
        }
        """.formatted(name, count);
// {
//   "name": "Alice",
//   "messageCount": 5
// }
```

**`formatted` vs `String.format`**:
- Same format spec; just instance method vs static.
- `formatted` reads naturally chained off the text block.
- Identical performance.

**Concatenation also works** (less clean):
```java
String json = """
        {
          "name": """ + name + "\"\n" +
        "}";  // ugly
```

**For complex templating**, use a dedicated library: **Mustache, Pebble, FreeMarker** — not text blocks + concat.

**Heredoc-style with parameters — the recommended pattern**:
```java
public String renderUserCard(User u) {
    return """
            <div class="user-card">
              <h3>%s</h3>
              <p>%s</p>
              <p>Joined: %s</p>
            </div>
            """.formatted(
                escapeHtml(u.getName()),
                escapeHtml(u.getEmail()),
                u.getJoinedAt().format(DATE_FMT));
}
```

**Why string templates were withdrawn**: the original design (`STR."Hello \{name}"`) had concerns around safety (SQL injection risk with naive use), processor lookup, and complexity. The JEP was pulled and a redesign is in progress. For now, **use `formatted`** for value injection.

**Spring Data JPA with text block in `@Query`**:
```java
@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    @Query(value = """
            SELECT u.* FROM users u
            JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id
            WHERE o.created_at >= :since
              AND u.active = true
            GROUP BY u.id
            HAVING COUNT(o.id) >= :minOrders
            ORDER BY u.created_at DESC
            """, nativeQuery = true)
    List<User> findActiveUsersWithRecentOrders(
        @Param("since") Instant since,
        @Param("minOrders") int minOrders);
}
```

**JSON test fixtures**:
```java
@Test
void shouldParseUserJson() throws Exception {
    String json = """
            {
              "id": 42,
              "name": "Alice",
              "email": "alice@example.com",
              "roles": ["admin", "user"]
            }
            """;
    User u = objectMapper.readValue(json, User.class);
    assertEquals(42L, u.id());
    assertEquals("Alice", u.name());
}
```

**HTML snippets in templating code**:
```java
String page = """
        <!DOCTYPE html>
        <html>
          <head><title>%s</title></head>
          <body>
            <h1>%s</h1>
            <p>%s</p>
          </body>
        </html>
        """.formatted(title, heading, body);
```

**GraphQL queries**:
```java
String query = """
        query GetUser($id: ID!) {
          user(id: $id) {
            id
            name
            email
            posts(limit: 10) {
              id
              title
            }
          }
        }
        """;
Map<String, Object> variables = Map.of("id", userId);
response = graphqlClient.execute(query, variables);
```

**Multi-line error messages**:
```java
throw new IllegalArgumentException("""
        Invalid configuration:
          - %s: %s (expected: %s)
          - file: %s
        Please check your configuration and try again.
        """.formatted(field, actualValue, expectedFormat, configFile));
```

A simple immutable data class in pre-Java-16:
```java
public final class UserDTO {
    private final Long id;
    private final String name;
    private final String email;

    public UserDTO(Long id, String name, String email) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
    }

    public Long getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public String getEmail() { return email; }

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof UserDTO that)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(id, that.id)
            && Objects.equals(name, that.name)
            && Objects.equals(email, that.email);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(id, name, email);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "UserDTO{id=" + id + ", name='" + name + "', email='" + email + "'}";
    }
}
```

With records (Java 16, JEP 395):
```java
public record UserDTO(Long id, String name, String email) {}
```

**That's the whole class**. The compiler generates everything: constructor, accessors, `equals`, `hashCode`, `toString`.

**Compared with Lombok `@Value`/`@Data`** — records don't need an annotation processor, work in the IDE without plugins, are recognized natively by tools (Jackson, JPA, etc.), and deconstruct in pattern matching (Java 21).

Given:
```java
public record UserDTO(Long id, String name, String email) {}
```

The compiler generates roughly:
```java
public final class UserDTO extends Record {
    private final Long id;
    private final String name;
    private final String email;

    public UserDTO(Long id, String name, String email) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.email = email;
    }

    public Long id()    { return id; }       // accessor (NOT getId())
    public String name() { return name; }
    public String email() { return email; }

    public boolean equals(Object o) { /* component-wise */ }
    public int hashCode()           { /* component-wise */ }
    public String toString()        { /* UserDTO[id=..., name=..., email=...] */ }
}
```

**Key points**:
- **`final` class** — cannot be subclassed.
- **Implicitly extends `Record`** — cannot extend another class.
- **Fields are `private final`** — immutable.
- **Accessors named after fields** — `name()` not `getName()`.
- **Component-wise equals/hashCode/toString** — based on declared components.

**Usage**:
```java
UserDTO user = new UserDTO(1L, "Alice", "alice@example.com");
System.out.println(user.name());   // Alice
System.out.println(user);          // UserDTO[id=1, name=Alice, email=alice@example.com]

UserDTO copy = new UserDTO(1L, "Alice", "alice@example.com");
user.equals(copy);   // true (value equality)
```

Records support a **compact constructor** — a special form that runs after parameters are received, before they're assigned to fields:

```java
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {
    // Compact constructor — no parameter list, no `this.x = x` assignments
    public Money {
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount, "amount must not be null");
        Objects.requireNonNull(currency, "currency must not be null");
        if (amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount cannot be negative: " + amount);
        }
        // Normalize: re-assign the parameter (the field will get the new value)
        currency = currency.toUpperCase();
        amount = amount.stripTrailingZeros();
    }
}

Money m = new Money(new BigDecimal("100.00"), "usd");
m.currency();  // "USD" (normalized)
m.amount();    // 1E+2 (stripped trailing zeros)
```

**Rules**:
- Compact constructor has **no parameter list** — the parameters are already in scope, named after the components.
- It **doesn't assign to `this.x`** — the assignment to fields happens automatically after the body runs.
- You can **reassign the parameter** to normalize — the field gets the new value.
- For validation, **throw** before the implicit assignment.

**Alternative: explicit canonical constructor** (rarely needed):
```java
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {
    public Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {
        Objects.requireNonNull(amount);
        this.amount = amount;
        this.currency = currency.toUpperCase();
    }
}
```
The compact form is cleaner; use the explicit form only if you need explicit assignments (very rare).

**Custom methods** — records can have any methods you want:
```java
public record Point(int x, int y) {
    public double distance(Point other) {
        return Math.hypot(x - other.x, y - other.y);
    }

    public Point translate(int dx, int dy) {
        return new Point(x + dx, y + dy);  // returns a new record (immutable)
    }
}
```

**Static factory methods** — named alternative to `new`:
```java
public record Money(BigDecimal amount, String currency) {
    public static Money usd(BigDecimal amount) {
        return new Money(amount, "USD");
    }

    public static Money zero(String currency) {
        return new Money(BigDecimal.ZERO, currency);
    }
}

Money m = Money.usd(new BigDecimal("100"));
```

**Implementing interfaces** — records can implement any interface:
```java
public interface Identifiable {
    Long getId();
}

public record ProductDTO(Long id, String name, BigDecimal price) implements Identifiable {
    @Override
    public Long getId() { return id; }  // satisfies the interface contract
}
```

**Static fields and methods** — fine; instance fields **NOT** allowed (besides components):
```java
public record Configuration(String name, int value) {
    public static final Configuration DEFAULT = new Configuration("default", 0);

    public static Configuration parse(String input) {
        // parsing logic
    }

    // ILLEGAL: cannot add instance fields
    // private int extra;
}
```

**Nested records** — useful for closely related data:
```java
public record OrderDetails(Long orderId, Customer customer, List<LineItem> items) {
    public record Customer(Long id, String name) {}
    public record LineItem(String sku, int qty, BigDecimal price) {}
}
```

**Non-canonical constructors** must delegate to the canonical:
```java
public record Range(int min, int max) {
    public Range(int max) {
        this(0, max);  // delegate to canonical
    }
}
```

Records pair perfectly with **sealed interfaces** (Java 17) for **algebraic data types** — closed hierarchies of data shapes:

```java
public sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}

public record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}
public record Square(double side) implements Shape {}
public record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {}
```

**Pattern matching on the closed hierarchy (Java 21)**:
```java
double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c   -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Square sq  -> sq.side() * sq.side();
        case Triangle t -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
        // No default needed — sealed hierarchy is exhaustive
    };
}
```

**Record deconstruction (Java 21, JEP 440)**:
```java
double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle(double r)         -> Math.PI * r * r;
        case Square(double side)      -> side * side;
        case Triangle(double b, double h) -> 0.5 * b * h;
    };
}
```

**Result type pattern**:
```java
public sealed interface Result<T> permits Result.Success, Result.Failure {
    record Success<T>(T value) implements Result<T> {}
    record Failure<T>(String error) implements Result<T> {}
}

String render(Result<User> r) {
    return switch (r) {
        case Result.Success<User>(var u) -> "User: " + u.name();
        case Result.Failure<User>(var e) -> "Error: " + e;
    };
}
```

This pattern is the Java equivalent of Scala's case classes + sealed traits, or Rust's enums with data.

**Jackson** (2.12+) recognizes records natively:
```java
public record CreateUserRequest(String name, String email) {}

@RestController
public class UserController {
    @PostMapping("/api/users")
    public UserDTO createUser(@RequestBody CreateUserRequest req) {
        // Jackson deserializes JSON into the record via its canonical constructor
        User saved = userService.create(req.name(), req.email());
        return new UserDTO(saved.getId(), saved.getName(), saved.getEmail());
    }
}
```

**Validation with Bean Validation** (since Jakarta Validation 3.0):
```java
public record CreateUserRequest(
    @NotBlank @Size(max = 100) String name,
    @Email @NotBlank String email
) {}
```

**Spring Data — query projections**:
```java
// DTO projection — record fields match column names
public record UserSummary(Long id, String name, String email) {}

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    @Query("SELECT new com.example.UserSummary(u.id, u.name, u.email) FROM User u WHERE u.active = true")
    List<UserSummary> findActiveSummaries();
}
// Or interface-based projection works too
```

**MapStruct** — generates record-to-entity mappings.

**JPA `@Entity`** — records do NOT work as entities (need no-arg constructor and mutable state). Use a class with `@Entity`, then map to a record DTO.

**Records in Maps and Sets** — work perfectly because of generated `equals`/`hashCode`:
```java
record PointKey(int x, int y) {}

Map<PointKey, String> labels = new HashMap<>();
labels.put(new PointKey(1, 2), "origin offset");
labels.get(new PointKey(1, 2));  // works correctly — value equality
```

Sealed types unlock **exhaustive switch expressions** (Java 21):

```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}
record Circle(double radius)         implements Shape {}
record Square(double side)           implements Shape {}
record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {}

double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c   -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Square sq  -> sq.side() * sq.side();
        case Triangle t -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
        // No `default` needed — all permitted subtypes covered
    };
}
```

**Add a new subtype → compile error in every switch**:
```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle, Hexagon {}  // added Hexagon
record Hexagon(double side) implements Shape {}

double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c   -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Square sq  -> sq.side() * sq.side();
        case Triangle t -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
        // COMPILE ERROR: Hexagon not covered
    };
}
```
The compiler tells you every place to update. **This is the maintenance superpower**.

**With record deconstruction** (Java 21, JEP 440):
```java
double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle(double r)              -> Math.PI * r * r;
        case Square(double side)           -> side * side;
        case Triangle(double b, double h)  -> 0.5 * b * h;
    };
}
```

**With `when` guards**:
```java
String classify(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c when c.radius() > 100 -> "big circle";
        case Circle c                       -> "small circle";
        case Square sq                      -> "square";
        case Triangle t                     -> "triangle";
    };
}
```

**Sealed type + records + pattern switch = ADTs in Java**, with the same expressive power as Scala case classes or Rust enums.

**Result type — functional error handling**:
```java
public sealed interface Result<T> permits Result.Success, Result.Failure {
    record Success<T>(T value) implements Result<T> {}
    record Failure<T>(String error, Throwable cause) implements Result<T> {}

    default <U> Result<U> map(Function<T, U> f) {
        return switch (this) {
            case Success<T>(var v)         -> new Success<>(f.apply(v));
            case Failure<T>(var e, var c)  -> new Failure<>(e, c);
        };
    }
}

Result<User> userResult = userService.find(id);
String display = switch (userResult) {
    case Result.Success<User>(var u)        -> "User: " + u.name();
    case Result.Failure<User>(var e, var c) -> "Error: " + e;
};
```

