**AST nodes for an interpreter**:
```java
public sealed interface Expr
    permits Expr.Lit, Expr.Var, Expr.Add, Expr.Mul, Expr.Let {

    record Lit(int value) implements Expr {}
    record Var(String name) implements Expr {}
    record Add(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}
    record Mul(Expr left, Expr right) implements Expr {}
    record Let(String name, Expr value, Expr body) implements Expr {}
}

int eval(Expr e, Map<String, Integer> env) {
    return switch (e) {
        case Expr.Lit(int v)         -> v;
        case Expr.Var(String n)      -> env.get(n);
        case Expr.Add(var l, var r)  -> eval(l, env) + eval(r, env);
        case Expr.Mul(var l, var r)  -> eval(l, env) * eval(r, env);
        case Expr.Let(var n, var v, var b) -> {
            var newEnv = new HashMap<>(env);
            newEnv.put(n, eval(v, env));
            yield eval(b, newEnv);
        }
    };
}
```

Before sealed types, the **Visitor pattern** was the way to do exhaustive double-dispatch:

```java
// Old visitor pattern
interface Shape {
    <R> R accept(Visitor<R> v);
}

interface Visitor<R> {
    R visit(Circle c);
    R visit(Square s);
    R visit(Triangle t);
}

class AreaVisitor implements Visitor<Double> {
    public Double visit(Circle c)   { return Math.PI * c.radius * c.radius; }
    public Double visit(Square s)   { return s.side * s.side; }
    public Double visit(Triangle t) { return 0.5 * t.base * t.height; }
}

double area(Shape s) {
    return s.accept(new AreaVisitor());
}
```

With sealed types + pattern matching:
```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Square, Triangle {}
record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}
record Square(double side) implements Shape {}
record Triangle(double base, double height) implements Shape {}

double area(Shape s) {
    return switch (s) {
        case Circle c   -> Math.PI * c.radius() * c.radius();
        case Square sq  -> sq.side() * sq.side();
        case Triangle t -> 0.5 * t.base() * t.height();
    };
}
```

**Sealed + pattern match wins**:
- One method, not a separate visitor class per operation.
- No `accept` boilerplate.
- New shapes are added in one place, not every visitor.
- Compile-time exhaustiveness for free.

**When the Visitor pattern still wins**:
- When you can't modify the data classes (they're sealed by someone else, or come from a library).
- When you have stateful traversal logic that's natural in a separate class.
- When the dispatch involves more than one type (true double-dispatch).

For most cases, **prefer sealed types + pattern matching**.

Pre-Java-14, an NPE in a chain like `a.getB().getC().getD()` told you almost nothing:

```java
String name = user.getDepartment().getManager().getName();
// java.lang.NullPointerException
//     at com.example.UserService.formatName(UserService.java:42)
```

**Which one was null?**
- `user`?
- `user.getDepartment()`?
- `user.getDepartment().getManager()`?

You had three suspects per chain. In real production code with deeper chains, this was even worse:
```java
request.getOrder().getItems().get(0).getProduct().getCategory().getParent().getName();
// NPE on line 99 — 7 possible nulls. Add logging, redeploy, hope you catch it.
```

This was a top complaint about Java for 25 years. **Helpful NPEs (JEP 358)** finally fixed it.

Java 14+ produces a **precise message naming the null sub-expression**:

```java
String name = user.getDepartment().getManager().getName();
// java.lang.NullPointerException:
//   Cannot invoke "com.example.Manager.getName()" because the return value
//   of "com.example.Department.getManager()" is null
//     at com.example.UserService.formatName(UserService.java:42)
```

**Now you know exactly**: `user.getDepartment()` returned a Department, but `getManager()` on it returned null.

**Examples of helpful messages**:

**Field access**:
```java
person.address.city.toUpperCase();
// Cannot invoke "String.toUpperCase()" because "person.address.city" is null
```

**Method chain**:
```java
user.getOrders().get(0).getTotal();
// Cannot invoke "java.math.BigDecimal.toString()" because the return value
// of "com.example.Order.getTotal()" is null
```

**Array access**:
```java
int[][] grid = ...;
int x = grid[0][1];
// Cannot load from object array because "grid[0]" is null
```

**Assignment**:
```java
person.address = newAddress;
// Cannot assign field "address" because "person" is null
```

**Unboxing**:
```java
Map<String, Integer> map = ...;
int count = map.get("missing");
// Cannot invoke "java.lang.Integer.intValue()" because the return value
// of "java.util.Map.get(Object)" is null
```

This is **enabled by default since Java 15** — no flags needed.
